  "response": "I’m using the repository’s `crimes-codebase-risk` skill because this task explicitly relies on interpreting `crimes context` findings. I’ll inspect the targeted file and context output, then propose behavior-preserving refactors without modifying the fixture.\n\nThe skill calls for JSON when planning and evidence-first interpretation. I’m now running the requested context command from the fixture root and reading the implementation so the proposed signatures preserve what each function actually does.\n\nThe global `crimes` binary is not installed in this environment (`command not found`). Per the repository instructions, I’m falling back to the locally built CLI and will use its JSON output if the build artifact is present.\n\n`crimes context` confirms the three requested low-severity findings. It also reports a related `boolean_naming_drift` on the negative flags. No likely tests were found, so each refactor should add focused contract tests.\n\n1. `option_bag_junk_drawer` — `describeCheckout`\n\nThe function reads six unrelated-looking properties from one broad bag. An agent adding a checkout attribute may append another field without knowing whether it belongs to identity, location, or execution state.\n\nRefactor into cohesive, explicitly named records and destructure at the boundary:\n\n```ts\ninterface CheckoutContext {\n  plan: string;\n  role: string;\n}\n\ninterface CheckoutLocale {\n  region: string;\n  currency: string;\n}\n\ninterface CheckoutState {\n  status: string;\n  shouldRetry: boolean;\n}\n\nexport function describeCheckout(\n  context: CheckoutContext,\n  locale: CheckoutLocale,\n  state: CheckoutState,\n): string {\n  const { plan, role } = context;\n  const { region, currency } = locale;\n  const { status, shouldRetry } = state;\n\n  return [\n    plan,\n    role,\n    region,\n    currency,\n    status,\n    String(shouldRetry),\n  ].join(\":\");\n}\n```\n\nThis ties each new field to an owned concept instead of turning `CheckoutOptions` into an ever-growing junk drawer.\n\n2. `return_shape_roulette` — `resolvePaymentResult`\n\nThe function returns three shapes with zero overlap between some variants and no explicit return type. An agent could access `result.ok`, `result.url`, or `result.value` as though it always existed, or add a fourth ambiguous shape.\n\nUse a named discriminated union:\n\n```ts\ntype PaymentResult =\n  | {\n      kind: \"error\";\n      code: string | undefined;\n      message: string | undefined;\n    }\n  | {\n      kind: \"redirect\";\n      url: string | undefined;\n      permanent: false;\n    }\n  | {\n      kind: \"success\";\n      value: string | undefined;\n      cached: false;\n    };\n\nexport function resolvePaymentResult(\n  input: PaymentResultInput,\n): PaymentResult {\n  if (input.error) {\n    return {\n      kind: \"error\",\n      code: input.code,\n      message: input.message,\n    };\n  }\n\n  if (input.redirect) {\n    return {\n      kind: \"redirect\",\n      url: input.url,\n      permanent: false,\n    };\n  }\n\n  return {\n    kind: \"success\",\n    value: input.value,\n    cached: false,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nCallers must now narrow on `kind`, while the explicit return type prevents future branches from silently inventing a new contract. A follow-up could also discriminate the input so `error` and `redirect` cannot both be true.\n\n3. `negative_flag_maze` — `canRunBilling`\n\n`!disableBilling && !skipRetry` requires two mental inversions. An agent extending it could apply De Morgan’s law incorrectly, negate only one flag, or misunderstand whether `skipRetry` permits billing.\n\nExpose positive states and return the predicate directly:\n\n```ts\ninterface BillingRunPolicy {\n  isBillingEnabled: boolean;\n  isRetryAllowed: boolean;\n}\n\nexport function canRunBilling({\n  isBillingEnabled,\n  isRetryAllowed,\n}: BillingRunPolicy): boolean {\n  return isBillingEnabled && isRetryAllowed;\n}\n```\n\nThis also resolves the related `boolean_naming